<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">United States Declaration of Independence</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1776">1776</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> Declaration of Independence was adopted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-continental-congress">Second Continental Congress</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> on 4 July 1776. Drafted mainly by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson">Thomas Jefferson</a>, it announced that the thirteen American colonies were independent states no longer bound to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, listed grievances against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-iii">George III</a> and set out the principle that governments rest on the consent of the governed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/united-states-declaration-of-independence.jpg" alt="United States Declaration of Independence" width="320" height="380" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: original: w:Second Continental Congress; reproduction: William Stone · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:United_States_Declaration_of_Independence.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/intolerable-acts">Coercive Acts</a> of 1774, called the Intolerable Acts in the colonies, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-continental-congress">First Continental Congress</a> met in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> and organised a boycott of British goods. The Revolutionary War began in April 1775, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-iii">George III</a> proclaimed the colonists to be in rebellion on 23 August 1775. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-continental-congress">Second Continental Congress</a> initially lacked clear authority to declare independence, since its delegates were bound by instructions from thirteen different governments. Historian Pauline Maier counts more than ninety state and local declarations of independence from April to July 1776. On 7 June Richard Henry Lee presented a resolution that the United Colonies ought to be free and independent states.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>On 11 June 1776 Congress appointed the Committee of Five, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-adams">John Adams</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-franklin">Benjamin Franklin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson">Thomas Jefferson</a>, Robert R. Livingston and Roger Sherman, to draft a declaration. Adams persuaded the committee to charge Jefferson with the first draft, which he largely wrote between 11 and 28 June and which Congress then edited. The text is often discussed as five parts: introduction, preamble, indictment of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-iii">George III</a>, denunciation of the British people, and conclusion. It cites 27 grievances against the king and asserts natural and legal rights, including a right of revolution. Jefferson's most immediate sources were his own draft of the preamble of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> constitution and George Mason's draft of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia-declaration-of-rights">Virginia Declaration of Rights</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Congress adopted the Declaration on 4 July 1776, and John Dunlap printed about 200 broadsides that night. The first public readings took place on 8 July in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a>, Trenton and Easton. The engrossed copy was ordered on 19 July and signed mostly on 2 August by fifty-six delegates. In the years after the Revolution the text drew little attention, and interest revived only in the 1790s with the first political parties. The French Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen of 1789 drew largely on American ideals. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham-lincoln">Abraham Lincoln</a> saw the Declaration as the moral standard to which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> should strive and made it the centrepiece of the Gettysburg Address in 1863.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “United States Declaration of Independence” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 4 July 1776 the Second Continental Congress adopted the <b>Declaration of Independence</b>, drafted mainly by Thomas Jefferson, which justified separation by appeal to natural rights.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-states">United States · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Historians have noted that the American Revolution and the <b>United States Declaration of Independence</b> may also have influenced them.</blockquote><a href="/movement/decembrist-revolt">Decembrist revolt · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>In America, Thomas Jefferson drafted the <b>United States Declaration of Independence</b> of 1776, and James Madison took a leading part in framing the constitution of 1787.</blockquote><a href="/movement/enlightenment">Enlightenment · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>The struggle against absolutism produced its opposites: the Bill of Rights 1689 in England, Locke’s theory of government by consent, Montesquieu’s separation of powers and the <b>United States Declaration of Independence</b>, which accused George III of seeking “an absolute Tyranny over these States”.</blockquote><a href="/government/absolute-monarchy">Absolute monarchy · Impact &amp; Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>John Locke’s Two Treatises of Government defined tyranny as “the exercise of power beyond right” and declared that “wherever law ends, tyranny begins”, and the <b>United States Declaration of Independence</b> charged George III with seeking “an absolute Tyranny”.</blockquote><a href="/government/tyranny">Tyranny · Impact &amp; Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>In January 1776 New Hampshire adopted a temporary constitution, the first of any colony, and joined the <b>Declaration of Independence</b> as a state of the United States.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-new-hampshire">Province of New Hampshire</a></li></ul></section>
